  "response": "The dispute at the centre of contemporary metaethics can be stated in a sentence. Moral realism holds that moral judgments describe objective moral facts; expressivism, by contrast, holds that moral judgments express the speaker's non-cognitive attitudes. Shorn of its technical apparatus, the realist thesis is simply that moral judgments report facts about the world, and the terms of the disagreement ensure that at least one of that thesis and the competing claim that moral judgments express the attitudes of the speaker who utters them must be correct, perhaps both together. Adjudication therefore turns on which side can accommodate everything that moral language is observed to do.\n\nMost of the pressure on the expressivist side arises from its semantic commitments. Since expressivism construes a moral verdict as the voicing of an attitude rather than the description of a state of affairs, it issues in non-cognitivism, which denies that moral sentences are truth-apt; in other words, on non-cognitivism moral utterances are incapable of being either true or false. That very incapacity generated the best-known objection to the programme. The Frege-Geach problem presses expressivists to explain the behaviour of moral terms embedded in conditionals, and that pressure led Simon Blackburn to develop quasi-realism to account for the apparently factual surface of moral discourse.\n\nThe realist camp has a repertoire of tests of its own, directed at the semantics of moral predicates rather than at the logic of embedded moral claims. G.E. Moore's open question argument targets analyses of goodness in purely naturalistic terms. The Moral Twin Earth thought experiment was introduced by Peter Railton. Neither tradition, however, yields a verdict from the armchair. Where the question is answered at all, it is answered about particular utterances in particular settings, by asking whether the words as used were verifiable, how a reasonable audience would have taken them, and what the surrounding context implied.\n\nThe law of defamation supplies the clearest example of such a case-by-case ruling, since there the difference between fact-stating and attitude-expressing speech carries practical consequences. Michael Milkovich sued the Lorain Journal over a column concerning his testimony at a hearing. The suit came first; the ruling that resolved it followed afterwards. The Supreme Court held in Milkovich v. Lorain Journal Co. that the First Amendment confers no wholesale defamation exemption on statements labelled as opinion. In its defamation jurisprudence the Court has established that any statement framed as opinion is categorically immune from defamation liability. Cutting against any blanket shield for opinion, the Court determined that the disputed passage in the column implied an assertion of objective fact about Milkovich's testimony, a claim about his veracity capable of being proved or disproved. What settled the question was thus not the label borne by the utterance but its content: a sentence that purports to say how things stood is fact-stating, however it is dressed.",
